  "findings": [
    "All-old ordered triples equal their parent values for every parameter. A triple with the newborn and two old labels not equal to j has its actual nonzero parent determinant at zero; continuity is applied to this specified triple only. The proof does not assume G1 for the whole duplicate-vertex tuple at zero.",
    "For a triple containing the newborn and old j, the exact area is -epsilon*det(q,P k-P j). For epsilon>0 admissibility supplies its nonzero factor. Reversed ordered triples use the actual SignType negation identities. This covers both attachment-label positions.",
    "The G1 helper exhausts each enlarged label as newborn or an actual old label. Repeated newborn cases contradict distinctness; permutations move a single newborn to the first position; three old labels use parent G1. No enlarged-label case or cyclic seam is omitted.",
    "The helper premise hnew is explicitly derived by a finite intersection over both old indices. The diagonal implication is vacuous only because G1 excludes repeated labels. Conversion from the resulting right-neighborhood to one positive interval gives the public conclusion for every epsilon in that interval.",
    "The public theorem assumes only parent G1 and the actual SoftAdmissible predicate, with n nonzero. This is broader than the source generic-parent domain, and in particular retains every n>=3 case. It introduces no genericity, sign-persistence, crossing or density oracle.",
    "All three preserved failure-to-final diffs affect proof terms only: SignType negation, explicit one-sided eventual typing, and Ne.symm. The nine theorem types are unchanged."
  ],
  "limits": "This group proves G1 on a common positive interval. It does not package full sign constancy, G2, one chamber, inherited crossings or Gauss-word claims. Those remain separate source obligations. Stronger fidelity approval and canonical integration remain pending. Hash and kernel checks do not establish source acceptance."
